course: dictionary
lesson: 7833
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The sentence tells you which meaning is right. «He met a rabbit cleaning his silly face with his paws» is about a real animal, so rabbit means a small furry creature.
explain: Rabbit can also be a verb in the UK. To rabbit on means to talk for a long time about things that do not matter. If someone says, «Stop rabbiting on», they want you to be quiet, not to hop away.
explain: Ask yourself which meaning fits. Put the meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. A rabbit can hop, but only a person can rabbit on.
fact: A rabbit is a small furry animal with long ears, strong back legs and a short fluffy tail.
fact: Rabbits are mammals, and they eat plants.
fact: «A rabbit's teeth grow all its life.» Without enough hay to chew, they can grow into sharp spikes, so vets check rabbits' teeth.
fact: Rabbit droppings are little round balls, and scientists can use droppings to learn which animals live in a place.
fact: To rabbit on (UK) means to talk for a long time about unimportant things.
exercise: «He met a rabbit cleaning his silly face with his paws.» What is the rabbit here? | A person who talks too much | A kind of hat | A long, unimportant story | *A small furry animal with long ears
exercise: Dad says, «Stop rabbiting on, we need to leave.» What does rabbiting on mean? | Eating a big meal of carrots | Hopping quickly across the garden | *Talking for a long time about unimportant things | Hiding in a burrow
exercise: A pet rabbit needs space and hay. Which fact is about its teeth? | It hops quickly across the lawn | It lives in a field on a farm | *Its teeth grow all its life, so it needs hay to chew | It has long ears and a short fluffy tail
test: Maths: Sam has 4 rabbits, and each rabbit has 4 legs. How many legs are there in total? | 8 | 12 | 20 | *16
test: Which sentence uses rabbit as a verb? | Dad bought two rabbits for the shop | *She rabbited on about her holiday for ages | The rabbit hopped under the fence | Rabbits have long ears
exam: In «Some pictures, like the duck-rabbit, can be seen in two ways», what does rabbit mean? | Someone who talks too much | A kind of duck | A small furry animal | *A picture that can look like a duck or a rabbit
